out.push(S('Stage 4R · Report (sample)'));
out.push(SAMPLE(['Mr Attar had written four hundred words saying the book was excellent, and somebody answered underneath asking which of the four he ought to buy: he had reviewed one book and the reader was holding four. Mrs Khoury said that is what a review is for and nobody is taught it — a review is not a judgement of a thing but a placing of a thing among the things a reader is choosing between, and everything else in it is decoration. Mrs Tarabishi asked even where you have not read the other three, and then you name the three and say which of them you have read, because a review that does not name the alternatives has handed the reader the only difficult part of the job. Dr Qabbani asked about the examination, and it is Writing Part 2, and the review is the task candidates do worst: they describe, they evaluate, and they forget the reader, when the rubric names the reader in every question it asks. Mr Tayara asked what naming the reader changes, and everything about which facts belong in it, because the sixty pages matter to a final-year student and not at all to a practising engineer, and Mr Attar rewrote the review for one named person and it lost a hundred words and answered the question. Mrs Oueidat asked about ranking, and you say which is best for whom, which is a different sentence: of the four, the only one that assumes no mathematics beyond the second year is a placing and a recommendation at once, and it ranks nothing. Mrs Ajouz asked whether that is not avoiding the question, and Mr Shihabi said a rank is one position and a placing is four — a reader who is told the best has to trust you, and a reader who is told what distinguishes the four can choose against your advice, and that is the reader who comes back. Mrs Rizk said her praise reads like advertising, and praise that costs nothing does, and the cure is a cost: it covers in sixty pages what the standard text covers in four hundred, and leaves out the two chapters you will need by the third month. Mr Zahreddine said that is the limit inside the claim again, and Mrs Oueidat said it is 4.5 in a fourth costume, and a limit inside the claim survives a hostile reader, a quoter, a summariser and now a reader choosing between four things, who is the least patient of the four. Mrs Tarabishi said her literature review is a review in this sense and she had not seen it, and it is exactly this at eleven pages instead of three hundred words. And Mr Attar said the thing he will take away is the question underneath: the reader was not asking whether the book was good, but asking him to spend his afternoon so that the reader would not have to spend his.']));
out.push(A('⚠ **Mr Attar\'s last sentence is the definition of the genre.** **The reader was not asking whether the book was good; he was asking him to spend his afternoon so that the reader would not have to spend his.**'));
out.push(S('Board clinic – typical mistakes'));
out.push(A('**1** ⚠ _An excellent book, strongly recommended._ → **true of all four and therefore of none.**   **2** _The best of the four._ → **a rank.** **Say which is best for whom.**   **3** _I would recommend it to anybody._ → ⚠ **names no reader, which is the fault the rubric is testing.**   **4** **Praise with no cost anywhere in the review** → **reads as advertising.** **One cost in the same paragraph repairs it.**'));
out.push(S('Stage 5 · Read'));
out.push(A('**1** ⚠ **A final-year student with no industrial experience who has one term and wants to answer a question about a real device at a first interview** — **and it is said in the first line, with an escape clause for everybody else.**'));
